#!/bin/sh
# Once pi's password is no longer the one the image was built with (changed
# with passwd, or set by Raspberry Pi Imager on first boot), stop asking for
# a new one: remove the marker and the sshd setting. Safe to run any time.
MARKER=/var/lib/zeroos/default-password
SSHD_CONF=/etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/10-zeroos-default-password.conf
[ -f "$MARKER" ] || exit 0
read -r user hash < "$MARKER"
now=$(awk -F: -v u="$user" '$1 == u {print $2}' /etc/shadow)
[ -n "$now" ] && [ "$now" != "$hash" ] || exit 0
rm -f "$MARKER" "$SSHD_CONF"
# New connections skip first-password now; open ones are not touched.
systemctl try-reload-or-restart ssh.service 2>/dev/null || true
echo "zeroos: password changed from the default; no longer asking"
